Under NC Medicaid’s **Orthotics & Prosthetics policy (5B)**, the policy specifically says the following are **not covered**:

- devices for the beneficiary’s **comfort or convenience**, or for the convenience of the beneficiary’s caregiver;
- **backup devices** or duplicate devices kept at different locations;
- devices and supplies for **residents of nursing facilities**;
- devices or supplies **covered by another agency**; and
- equipment or supplies for beneficiaries receiving **hospice care**.  
  [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), §§ 4.2, 4.2.1]

## General criteria that must be met for coverage

For coverage under Policy 5B, orthotic or prosthetic items must generally meet these requirements:

- be **ordered by a physician, physician assistant, or nurse practitioner**;
- be **medically necessary** to maintain or improve the beneficiary’s medical, physical, or functional level;
- be appropriate for use in a **noninstitutional setting** where normal life activities take place;
- be supported by a **documented face-to-face encounter** with the ordering clinician no more than **6 months** before initiation of the orthotic/prosthetic item; and
- have the beneficiary’s need **reviewed at least annually** by the ordering clinician.  
  [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), §§ 3.2, 3.2.1]

The policy adds that:

- **orthotic devices** are medically necessary when needed to correct or prevent skeletal deformities, support or align movable body parts, or preserve or improve physical function;
- **prosthetic devices** are medically necessary when they replace all or part of the function of a permanently inoperative, absent, or malfunctioning body part, and the beneficiary requires the prosthesis for **mobility, daily care, or rehabilitation**;
- the device must be a **reasonable and medically necessary** part of the treatment plan;
- it must be **consistent with the diagnosis and medical condition**, especially the beneficiary’s functional limitations and symptoms; and
- it must be of **high quality**, with replacement parts available and obtainable.  
  [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), §§ 3.2, 3.2.1]

## Separate general exclusions

Even if an item is otherwise the kind of device addressed by Policy 5B, it is not covered if:

- the beneficiary does not meet the **eligibility requirements** in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does not meet the **coverage criteria** in Section 3.0;
- the item or service **duplicates** another provider’s procedure, product, or service; or
- it is **experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial**.  
  [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 4.1]

## Practical bottom line

NC Medicaid does **not** cover O&P items that are mainly for convenience, backup, nursing-facility residents, hospice beneficiaries, or items another agency covers. Coverage generally requires a proper order, medical necessity, recent face-to-face documentation, annual review, and consistency with the beneficiary’s diagnosis and treatment plan. The current authoritative document is **Policy 5B, Orthotics & Prosthetics**; I checked for amendments and found none in the corpus for this document.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must be provided under a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the plan period;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the treatment plan. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

Related 10A requirements also say:

- **All services must be provided according to a treatment plan** that meets those requirements.
- Providers must **review and renew or revise treatment plans and goals at least every six calendar months**. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

For documentation, the beneficiary record must include **a copy of the treatment plan**. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)

This reflects the current authoritative version of **Policy 10A**, effective **February 1, 2025**.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and service plan, and they must be aimed at improving the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. The beneficiary also agrees to work collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, and technology. Current authority is the 9/1/2025 version of Policy 3K-2, with no later amendment returned in the amendment check. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA covers equipment, modification, and technology when it is intended to:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve the beneficiary’s environmental and community accessibility; or
- address the beneficiary’s 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy’s home-modification examples include, among other things:

- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- medically necessary emergency egress modifications;
- bathroom modifications for accessibility;
- bedroom and kitchen modifications to accommodate disability-related needs;
- certain non-skid surfaces, lifts, door modifications, and vehicle-related accessibility modifications;
- smart-home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- certain air filtration systems and backup generators when specific medical criteria are met.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The residence/vehicle limits in the policy are also important. Modifications may apply to:

- the beneficiary’s primary private residence if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence when the modifications are portable;
- a rented residence when the owner allows the modification; and
- the beneficiary’s primary vehicle, or the primary vehicle of the documented legal guardian or primary caregiver.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## What the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment, technology, and home modification requests, the MDT/documentation must include:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested assistive technology, equipment, or home modification.
- **The cost of the item** in the recommendation.
- **A plan for training** the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment; the case manager must document that training was completed, and it must be signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party.
- **A statement of need in the service plan.**
- **Evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable.
- **Itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request.
- **Any other information required for the specific equipment requested.**
- **Quotes**, when required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number required by NC Medicaid.
- **Written recommendations from appropriate professionals** when NC Medicaid or its designee determines special circumstances require them.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also specifically requires documentation maintained for equipment/supply/adaptation/modification requests, including:

- **a comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- **a copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- **a recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- **the estimated life of the equipment** and the expected length of time the beneficiary will benefit from it; and
- **an invoice from the supplier** showing the date provided and the cost, with related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Practical summary

For a CAP/DA home modification to be approved, the record should show:

1. the modification is disability-related and fits one of the program purposes;
2. it is for an allowable home/vehicle setting;
3. the need is identified by the MDT and reflected in the service plan;
4. medical necessity/supporting professional documentation is included when applicable; and
5. required cost, quote, training, and supplier documentation is provided.  
   [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

If you want, I can also pull out the **non-covered/excluded home modification items** under CAP/DA into a short checklist.

Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must be certified and continue meeting both organizational and staffing standards; the provider can be decertified for specified compliance, staffing, documentation, billing, confidentiality, and legal violations.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid policy requires an HIV case management provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of 3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the policy’s staff qualifications;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervisor qualifications;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet all applicable **state and federal laws** governing Medicaid participation;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**.  
In addition, within **1 year of Medicaid enrollment**, the provider must achieve national accreditation from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.1, effective 2020-01-15, current per amendment check.

### Recertification
For recertification, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of items from the initial certification packet that have changed;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- undergo a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of beneficiary records or other business/clinical documentation as needed.  
[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.4, effective 2020-01-15, current per amendment check.

### What the recertification checklist specifically asks for
The NC Medicaid recertification checklist reflects these operational requirements. It asks, among other things, whether the application:

- covers **only one physical location**;
- includes the title page and signed checklist;
- lists all **case managers** and **supervisors** with employment dates;
- lists all **counties served**;
- lists all services provided;
- states **operating hours** and the **emergency after-hours plan**;
- gives the **accrediting agency name and dates** or an explanation;
- states the frequency of the **satisfaction survey** and how results are used; and
- lists the numbers of **Medicaid and Ryan White clients**.  
[dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf), current per amendment check.

## When a provider can be decertified

A provider may be decertified for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing** practices;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** for cited unmet requirements, or make recommended corrections, within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the qualifications in **Subsection 6.1**;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units** under the policy;
- failure to provide case management staff with supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the time required by the policy, NC Medicaid, or its designated contractor;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, as required by **§ 7.5**;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of agency changes, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in **§ 6.2**.  
[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.5, effective 2020-01-15, current per amendment check.

## Related documentation/training requirements tied to certification risk

Two related requirements matter because failure to meet them can support decertification:

- The policy requires specific beneficiary record documentation, including proof of HIV status, assessments, care plans, progress notes, consent forms, physician orders showing diagnosis and medical necessity, and documentation supporting billed services. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 7.5, effective 2020-01-15, current per amendment check.
- Annual staff training includes confidentiality, cultural competency, HIV disease management trends, ethics, refresher training on core case management components, and treatment of people living with HIV; **6 of the 12 annual hours** must be clinically oriented. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.1.7.2, effective 2020-01-15, current per amendment check.

## Bottom line

To stay certified, an HIV case management provider must maintain qualified staff and supervision, site enrollment, accreditation, quality improvement, documentation, and timely reporting/compliance. Decertification can occur for failures in service delivery, staffing, training, supervision, documentation, billing integrity, confidentiality, legal compliance, corrective action, or maintaining the certification standards themselves.
